---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import ContentSection from '../../components/ContentSection.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import LinkCard from '../../components/LinkCard.astro';

const relatedPages = [
  { title: 'College Basketball Hub', href: '/college-basketball-betting', description: 'Complete CBB betting guide' },
  { title: 'Conference Tournaments', href: '/college-basketball/conference-tournaments', description: 'Pre-March wagering' },
  { title: 'Big Games & Tournaments', href: '/big-games-tournaments', description: 'High-profile event dynamics' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="March Madness Betting Guide"
  description="How to approach NCAA Tournament betting, market dynamics during March Madness, common mistakes, and factors that matter in tournament wagering."
>
  <Hero
    title="March Madness Betting Guide"
    subtitle="NCAA Tournament Betting Considerations and Market Dynamics"
    size="medium"
  />

  <div class="container content-container">
    <article class="page-content">
      <ContentSection>
        <div class="quick-take">
          <h2>Quick Take</h2>
          <p>
            March Madness is the most bet sporting event in the United States after the Super Bowl. The single-elimination format, casual betting participation, and bracket-pool mentality create unique market dynamics. Lines are generally sharp due to volume, but public bias toward popular programs, seed-based assumptions, and narrative betting can create opportunities. The key is understanding how the tournament format changes the game.
          </p>
        </div>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="How Does March Madness Differ from Regular Season?" id="differences">
        <h3>Single elimination changes everything</h3>
        <p>
          Regular season losses are survivable. Tournament losses end your season. This affects team psychology, coaching decisions, and market dynamics. Teams play with different urgency and risk tolerance.
        </p>

        <h3>Neutral sites</h3>
        <p>
          Most tournament games are at neutral locations, removing traditional home court advantage. Site proximity matters—a team playing 100 miles from campus has more fans than one playing 2,000 miles away. But it's not the same as true home court.
        </p>

        <h3>Compressed schedule</h3>
        <p>
          Teams can play 3 games in 5 days (first weekend) or 2 games in 3 days. Fatigue, depth, and recovery become factors that don't exist in regular season analysis.
        </p>

        <h3>Massive casual betting participation</h3>
        <p>
          More casual bettors enter the market during March Madness than any other time. Bracket pools encourage people to bet on games they've barely analyzed. This influx changes market composition.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What Does the Market Often Get Wrong?" id="market-errors">
        <Callout type="info" title="Volume Makes Lines Sharp—But Not Perfect">
          <p>
            The betting volume on March Madness creates efficient markets. But systematic biases can still exist, particularly around seed perceptions and name recognition.
          </p>
        </Callout>

        <h3>Seed-based assumptions</h3>
        <p>
          Public bettors often assume a 3-seed "should" beat a 14-seed by a large margin. But seeds reflect committee perception, not precise power ratings. Seed gaps don't always translate to point spread accuracy.
        </p>

        <h3>Name recognition bias</h3>
        <p>
          Duke, Kentucky, Kansas, and North Carolina get overbet relative to their current-year quality. A weak Duke team might be more popular with bettors than a strong mid-major because of brand recognition.
        </p>

        <h3>Cinderella narratives</h3>
        <p>
          After an upset, the underdog becomes a media darling. Public money pours in on their next game. But surviving one round doesn't make a team better—they might have just been underpriced once.
        </p>

        <h3>Fatigue and depth underrating</h3>
        <p>
          Teams with shorter rotations struggle in the second weekend. The market sometimes underrates how much Saturday-Sunday back-to-backs affect teams that rely heavily on their starters.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What This Means for Bettors" id="what-means">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Separate bracket pools from betting:</strong> Your bracket doesn't need to match your bets. They're different games with different optimal strategies.</li>
          <li><strong>Evaluate current-year quality:</strong> Ignore seed and name. What does the data say about these specific teams this season?</li>
          <li><strong>Consider matchups over records:</strong> Tournament matchups are often stylistic clashes that don't reflect regular season competition.</li>
          <li><strong>Factor in rest and travel:</strong> Who played last? Who traveled farther? Who has the deeper bench?</li>
          <li><strong>Be selective:</strong> With 67 games, you don't need to bet them all. Wait for spots where analysis provides confidence.</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Common Misconceptions" id="misconceptions">
        <h3>"Always bet first round upsets"</h3>
        <p>
          12-over-5 upsets happen regularly, but the market knows this. Lines on 12-seeds account for upset probability. Blindly betting underdogs isn't a strategy.
        </p>

        <h3>"Experienced coaches win in March"</h3>
        <p>
          Coaching matters, but "tournament experience" is overrated as a predictive factor. Young coaches win titles too. Don't overweight coaching resume.
        </p>

        <h3>"The best teams always advance"</h3>
        <p>
          Single elimination is inherently volatile. The best team wins the title maybe 20-25% of the time. Variance is not an error—it's the format.
        </p>

        <h3>"My bracket analysis transfers to betting"</h3>
        <p>
          Brackets and betting have different incentives. In brackets, you need upsets to differentiate. In betting, you need to find value against the spread. Different games.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="When This Matters Most" id="when-matters">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>First round:</strong> Most games, most casual betting, potentially softer lines</li>
          <li><strong>Second weekend:</strong> Fatigue and depth become major factors</li>
          <li><strong>Sweet 16/Elite 8:</strong> Better teams remain, tighter matchups</li>
          <li><strong>Final Four:</strong> Maximum public attention, name recognition bias peaks</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Decision Checklist" id="checklist">
        <p>Before betting any March Madness game:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Am I betting based on current-year analysis or name/seed recognition?</li>
          <li>Have I considered the stylistic matchup, not just overall records?</li>
          <li>Am I factoring in rest, travel, and schedule effects?</li>
          <li>Is this a genuine edge or am I just betting because it's March?</li>
          <li>Am I letting my bracket influence my betting decisions?</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Key Takeaways" id="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>Single elimination changes team behavior and market dynamics</li>
          <li>Casual betting volume increases—both creating and masking inefficiencies</li>
          <li>Seeds and names are overweighted; current-year quality matters more</li>
          <li>Fatigue and depth become crucial in later rounds</li>
          <li>Separate your bracket strategy from your betting strategy</li>
          <li>Be selective—67 games means many opportunities to pass</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Related Resources" id="related">
        <div class="related-grid">
          {relatedPages.map((page) => (
            <LinkCard
              title={page.title}
              href={page.href}
              description={page.description}
            />
          ))}
        </div>
      </ContentSection>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-content {
    padding: var(--spacing-3xl) 0;
  }

  .quick-take {
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent);
    padding: var(--spacing-lg);
    border-radius: 0 var(--border-radius) var(--border-radius) 0;
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-xl);
  }

  .quick-take h2 {
    font-size: 1rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-sm);
  }

  .quick-take p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  .related-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: var(--spacing-md);
    margin-top: var(--spacing-lg);
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .related-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
